1st-century skills that are vital for their future success.\nWe extend our heartfelt thanks to educators, parents, and most importantly, our students for joining us on this educational journey. Together, we are making computer education an exciting and empowering experience.\nWe welcome your suggestions for improving the content, which will be considered for future editions.\n— Publishers","21st Century Skills\nNATIONAL EDUCATION POLICY (NEP) 2020\nThe National Education Policy(NEP) 2020, as envisaged by the Ministry of Education, aims to provide high quality education and educational oppurtunities to an India that has more than one-third of its population under 20. The importance of NEP 2020 for such a critical demographic group cannot be emphasised enough.\nThroughout our books series, we have tried to ensure that the objectives of NEP 2020 are realised and achieved.\nUN SDGs\nEnsuring the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are supported and met by 2030\nMultilingualism and Culture\nPromoting learning of multiple languages, and understanding Indian heritage and culture.\nCognitive Capabilities\nDeveloping cognitive capacities (literacy, numeracy, critical thinking and problem solving).\nClearity of Concepts\nEmphasising cocepts understanding rather than role learing and learning-for-exams.\nEmotional Development\nDeveloping social, ethical, and emotional capacities and dispositions.\nCreativity\nDeveloping creative imagination with sound ethical moorings and values.\nExperiential Learning\nLearning by doing, nurturing rational thought and action, compassion and empathy, courage and resilience and developing a scientific temper.\nTechnical Proficiency through Integrated Solutions\nPromoting the extensive use of available technology to make learning more immersive.","The integration of NEP 2020 and NCF 2023 ensures that the education system recognizes and supports the holistic needs of students, fostering their well-being and creating an engaging and enjoyable learning experience through the implementation of these concepts.\nApplication of Knowledge\nrefers to the available knowledge being used to make decisions and perform tasks.\nCollaboration\nrefers to the ability to work together with others as a team.\nCritical Thinking\nrefers to objective analysis and evaluation of an issue in order to form a judgement.\nCommunication\nrefers to the ability to express one's opinions, needs and feelings appropriately, both verbally and non verbally.\nArt Integration\nrefers to using the art form to aid teaching and learning and enhance the understanding of the subject.\nCreative Thinking\nrefers to the ability to consider something in a novel way. it includes analysis and gaining new insights.","CONTENTS\n1. Data Storage and Memory 7\n2. Formatting in MS Word-2016 15\n• Periodic Worksheet - 1 26\n3. Computer Virus & Cyber Crime 27\n4. PowerPoint-2016 Presentation 35\n• Test Paper - 1 47\n5. GUI Operating System- Desktop Management 48\n6. Introduction to Spreadsheet 59\n• Periodic Worksheet - 2 66\n7. More About Internet 67\n8. Blocks in Scratch 74\n• Periodic Worksheet - 3 84\n• Robotics and AI 85\n• Game Name-Song Maker Using Google Experiments 89\n• Project 91\n• Test Paper - 2 92\n• Cyber Olympiad Paper 94","1\nData Storage\nand Memory\nComputer memory is like the computer's brain. It helps the computer to remember things. Just like we remember our favourite stories or games, the computer needs memory to remember its tasks and information.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nUnderstand the meaning of data and information.\nKnow about computer memory and its types.\nLearn types of storage devices and their uses.\nNiha : Tia, Have you ever wondered how a computer remembers all our games and pictures?\nTia : I'm not sure, Niha. How does it do that?\nNiha : Well, it's like how we keep our toys safe in boxes, a computer has a storage place.\nTia : Oh, I get it, Niha. So, the computer has special places to keep its things?\nNiha : Exactly, Tia! We'll learn more about it and how it works.\nTia : That sounds interesting, Niha. Let's explore it together!\nData vs Information\nData refers to raw facts in unprocessed form. The word \"data\" is a plural form of \"datum\".\nWhen data is processed, organized, structured or presented in a meaningful way, then it is called as \"Information\".\nIt is not enough to have data. Data themselves is useless. But when this data is processed, it becomes useful and can be called as Information.\n7\nSuper Computers-4","Example :\nTeacher wants to find highest and lowest scorers in class input marks Data CPU Processed Input data Information\nData Converted into Information\nWe know that CPU is the brain of a computer. It controls all the functions of a computer. It processes data and gives results.\nComputers can store large amounts of data. We can retrieve the data as we need it.\nInput Process Output Storage\nFlowchart of Processing\nNow, we will learn about memory and various storage devices.\nMEMORY\nComputer memory is very similar to the human brain. It is used to store data and instructions given to it. It can store the information either temporarily or permanently.\nIn computers, memory is a device that is used to store information.\nA computer can store the program in its memory. When a certain program starts on the computer, first of all, it is loaded into the computer's memory. A computer can load many programs in its memory. Faster the memory of the computer is, faster is the speed of opening of the programs.\nTYPES OF MEMORY\nThe computer memory can be classified into the following types:\n1. Primary memory\n2. Secondary memory\n8\nSuper Computers-4","The classification of computer memory is shown in the following figure :\nComputer Memory\nPrimary Memory\nSecondary Memory\nRAM\nROM\nHard disk\nCD\nDVD\nPen drive\nClassification of Types of Memory\n1. Primary or Internal memory : Primary memory is also known as the internal or main memory. A computer cannot run without the primary memory. It is used to store data or information that the CPU needs to access directly and quickly.\nThe Primary memory can be divided into two parts :\nRAM (Random Access Memory)\nROM (Read only Memory)\nRAM: When we refer to the computer's primary memory, we actually refer to RAM. It is used by the computer to store data for processing. If the computer is switched off, the data in RAM will be lost. Hence, RAM is referred to as volatile memory.\nRam\nROM: It stores permanent instructions required for the proper functioning of a computer system. These instructions are stored by the manufacturer of the computer. ROM is a non-volatile memory, as data stored in it is not lost, even when the computer is switched off.\nRom\n9\nSuper Computers-4","2. Secondary Memory : The primary memory is limited and temporary. Therefore, there is a need for secondary memory that can be used to store large amounts of data permanently. The data remains there even when the power to the computer is switched off.\nSome commonly used secondary storage device are as follows :\n1. Hard Disk\n2. Compact Disc\n3. DVD (Digital Versatile Disk)\n4. Pen drive\n5. Memory Card\nHard Disk : A hard disk is the main secondary storage device. A hard disk is a device fixed inside the CPU box. It can store a huge amount of data. All the work done on a computer can be stored on a hard disk. We can read and write data on this device any number of times.\nTYPES OF HARD DISKS\nLet us see different types of hard disks.\nINTERNAL HARD DISK\nThe internal hard disk is the main secondary storage device that is permanently installed inside the computer. It can store a huge amount of data that includes the operating system, application programs and other data, including images, documents, music and videos on a computer. The hard disk is usually given a label such as C: or D: called as C drive or D drive.\nFig. 1.7 Hard Disk\nEXTERNAL HARD DISK\nAn external hard disk is a removable hard disk that connects to a computer through a USB connector. You can plug in an external hard disk into multiple computers and use it as a removable storage device. An external hard disk is, however, slower than an internal hard disk.\nExternal Hard Disk\nCD\nA compact disc is a type of optical media. It can store up to 700 MB of data. These are circular in shape and are used mainly for storing audio-visual data in digital form.\nCompact-Disk\nDVD\nDVD is another type of optical media used for storing digital data such as videos, movies and softwares. It is similar to a CD, but it can store ten times more data than a CD. The video quality of a DVD is also much better than that of a CD.\nDVD\n10\nSuper Computers-4","Pendrive : A pendrive is a storage device which is small in size and can be easily carried in a pocket. It can store large amounts of data. It is mostly used to transfer data quickly and efficiently from one computer to another computer. It has to be inserted into a computer's USB port to store or retrieve information from it.\nPen Drive\nMemory Card : A memory card is a very small compact storage device. It is mainly used in cameras, music players, and smartphones to store pictures, songs, movies, etc.\nMemory Card\nMEMORY UNITS\nData stored in the memory is measured in the units such as bits, bytes, kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes, terabytes and petabytes.\nBit: A bit (short for binary digit) is the smallest unit of data in a computer. A bit has a single binary value, either 0 or 1.\nByte: Combination of 8 bits. one byte stores one character such as any letter 'k', 'a' or any other symbol like @, $, etc.\nFun Task (Critical Thinking)\n1. What is Data? How is it different from information?\n2. What is DVD?\nNibble : Half a byte (four bits) is called Nibble.\nThe bits in a byte are normally numbered from zero to seven using the convention as shown below :\n7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0\nMEASURING THE COMPUTER'S MEMORY\nOne file can be of thousands of bytes. So, instead of using bytes to measure data, we can also use higher units such as kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes, terabytes and petabytes.\nBoth computer memory and disk space are measured in these units.\n1 Bit = Binary Digit (0 or 1)\n1 Byte = 8 Bits\n1024 Bytes = 1 Kilo Byte\n11\nSuper Computers-4","1024 Kilo Bytes (KB) = 1 Mega Byte\n1024 Mega Bytes (MB) = 1 Giga Byte\n1024 Giga Bytes (GB) = 1 Tera Byte\n1024 Tera Bytes (TB) = 1 Peta Byte\n1024 Peta Bytes (PB) = 1 Exa Byte\n1024 Exa Bytes (EB) = 1 Zetta Byte\n1024 Zetta Bytes (ZB) = 1 Yotta Byte\nReBOOT\nYou enter data and instruction into the computer using input devices.\nThe data and instructions are stored inside the computer in some storage devices.\nPrimary memory and secondary memory are the two types of memory.\nSome commonly used secondary devices are Hard disk, Pen drive, DVD, CD, etc.\nPrimary memory is of two types- RAM and ROM.\nThe data stored on the computers is handled in terms of bits.\nBRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of words given in the box.\nPrimary Nibble Bit RAM\n1. Half a byte is called ____________.\n2. ____________ is the smallest amount of data.\n3. CPU directly retrieves data from the __________.\n4. The __________ memory comprises of RAM and ROM.\nB. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. 1 Byte is equal to 8 Bits.\n2. ROM is a temporary memory.\n3. RAM is permanent memory.\n4. RAM is faster than any other memory.\n12\nSuper Computers-4","C. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. _____________ is the smallest unit of data and has either a value of 0 or 1.\n(i) Byte (ii) Bit\n(iii) Nibble (iv) Kilobyte\n2. _____________ holds the instructions given by the manufacturer to operate the computer.\n(i) RAM (ii) ROM\n(iii) PEN DRIVE (iv) Hard disk.\n3. Which of the following stores the data permanently?\n(i) Hard disk (ii) Memory\n(iii) Motherboard (iv) Pen drive\n4. The word \"data\" is a plural form of _____________.\n(i) Datum (ii) Datam\n(iii) Datume (iv) Data\nD. Answer the following questions.\n1. What is a bit?\n2. What is Primary Memory?\n3. Differentiate between RAM and ROM.\n4. What is computer memory?\n13\nSuper Computers-4","EXECUTE TIME (Subject Enrichment)\n1. Riya has created a document on “Global Warming” with the help of her parents at home. Now she wants to show it to her teacher. Can you suggest to her which device she can use to store the document?\n2. Suraj wants to know how files are measured on the computer. Can you help him how?\nACTIVITY TIME\n1. Make a list of the storage devices that you use at your home. Describe the use of each device.\n2. Draw your favourite cartoon in Paint. Add colour and text to the shapes. Save it on a pen drive.\n3. Calculate and compare the storage capacity of a USB drive and a DVD. Which one can hold more data?\n4. Find the storage capacity of the following :\nRAM : ROM :\nPen drive : Hard Disk :\nGroup Discussion\nDiscuss about the types of computer storage devices.\nLet's Explore\nWith the help of your teacher, find out how you can view the memory size of various storage devices of a computer. Find out the memory size of the RAM and hard disk of your computer in the lab and at home, and then compare them.\nFor Educators\nTake the students to the computer lab and show them input devices, output devices, and storage devices you find there.\n14\nSuper Computers-4","2\nFormatting in\nMS Word 2016\nConcept Note\nMicrosoft Word is a widely used word-processing program developed by Microsoft Corporation. It is used for creating, editing, and formatting text documents.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nChange the font, size and case of text.\nChange the line spacing in a paragraph.\nAdd image files into a document.\nIncrease or decrease the indent of a paragraph.\nAlign a paragraph in different ways.\nNiha : Tia, today we're going to learn about formatting in MS Word.\nTia : What is formatting, Niha?\nNiha : It's like making our text appealing on the computer. We can change the colors, make text big or small, and add special styles. We can also insert images and shapes in your text.\nTia : Wow, Niha, that sounds like fun! Let's learn how to make our text appealing!\nFORMATTING TEXT\nChanging the style, size and colour of text is called as text formatting.\nLet's learn to create a well-formatted document using the Font, Font size and Change Case features of MS Word.\nYou can format your text using the options present in the Font section on the Home tab.\n15\nSuper Computers-4","CHANGING THE FONT OF TEXT\nA font is a collection of characters in a specific style or design. When you open MS Word and start typing, the default font is 'Calibri (Body)' and font size is '11'. MS Word provides us with a variety of fonts that we can use for the text.\nFor a document to look attractive and presentable, different types of text should be in different sizes and colours. For example, a heading is usually bigger than normal text. You can easily select text and change its size.\nSteps to change the font of text are as follows:\n1. Select the text.\n2. Click the drop-down arrow present next to the Font box. A menu of font styles will appear.\n3. Select the desired font from the list.\n4. The desired font is applied to the selected text.\nCHANGING THE SIZE OF TEXT\nSteps to change the size of text :\n1. Select the text that you want in different font sizes.\n2. Click the drop-down arrow present next to the Font Size box.\n3. Select the desired font size from the list.\n4. The selected font size is applied to the text.\n16\nSuper Computers-4","CHANGING THE CASE OF TEXT\nThe case describes how each letter is capitalized within a word or phrase. When you open MS Word and start typing, the default font case is 'lower case'. MS Word provides us with a variety of font cases that we can use for the text.\nSentence case : It makes the first letter of each sentence capitalized.\nlowercase : It changes the entire text from capital letters to small letters.\nUPPERCASE: It changes all the letters in a text from small to capital letters.\nCapitalize Each Word: It makes the first letter of each word capitalized.\ntOGGLEcASE: It allows you to shift between two cases.\nSteps to change the font case to Uppercase :\n1. Select the text\n2. Click the drop-down arrow beside the Change Case option.\n3. Select the UPPERCASE font case to change the text to capital letters.\nThe selected text appears as shown above in Uppercase.\nActivity-1\ninter Disciplinary\n1. Open MS Word.\n2. Write a short note on \"Water Resources\".\n3. Give a heading \"Water Resources\".\n4. Change the font and font size of the paragraph.\nFORMATTING OF A PARAGRAPH\nINDENTATION\nYou know that a paragraph is a series of sentences. You can have many paragraphs in a Word document. All paragraphs usually have the same spacing between the left and right margins of a page. However, sometimes you may want to increase or decrease the spacing\n17\nSuper Computers-4","between a paragraph and the margins. You can do this by using the Paragraph Indentation.\nDecrease Indent\nIncrease Indent\nYou can decrease or increase the spacing between a paragraph and the page margins by using the Decrease Indent or Increase Indent options.\nDecrease Indent : It moves the paragraph towards the left margin by a fixed distance.\nIncrease Indent : It moves the paragraph away from the left margin and towards the right margin by a fixed distance.\nSteps to indent a paragraph :\n1. Select the paragraph that you want to indent.\n2. Click the Increase Indent icon in the Paragraph section of the Home tab.\nThe selected paragraph is shifted to the right.\nLINE SPACING\nLine spacing is the blank space between the lines of a paragraph in a document. You can increase or decrease this line spacing.\nSteps to add line spacing to a paragraph :\n1. Select the paragraph.\n2. Click the drop-down arrow present next to the Line and Paragraph Spacing icon and select a number from the list.\n18\nSuper Computers-4","3. The selected line spacing is applied to the selected paragraph.\nALIGNMENT\nAlignment refers to how the text is placed in a document in relation to the margins. You can set four types of alignment such as, left, right, center or justify for a paragraph in MS Word.\nAlign Left\nAlign Right\nCentre\nJustify\nAlign Left\nIt places the selected text in line with the left margin. By default, when you type some text in Word, it is always left aligned.\nCenter\nIt places the selected text at the center of the left and right margins. It is mostly used for titles or headings.\nAlign Right\nIt places the selected text in line with the right margin. It is often used for placing dates or page numbers in a document.\nJustify\nIt places the text evenly between the left and right margins by adding extra spaces between words. It is mainly used while designing books, magazines and newspapers to give a uniform and neat look to the text by avoiding a ragged right edge.\n19\nSuper Computers-4","Steps to change the alignment of a paragraph :\n1. Select the paragraph.\n2. Click the Center align icon from the Paragraph section.\n3. The selected paragraph is aligned at the center of the page.\nINSERTING IMAGES\nIn MS Word, you can add images and shapes to make your document more attractive. In MS Word 2016, you can insert images in two ways— by using This Device option or Online Pictures option. Clip Art is a collection of pictures or images that can be imported into a document. It is normally organised into categories, such as people, objects, nature, etc., which is helpful when searching through thousands of images.\nINSERTING IMAGES USING ONLINE PICTURE OPTIONS\nSteps to add Online Pictures are as follows :\n1. Place your cursor in a document where you want to insert the picture.\n2. Click on the Insert tab.\n3. Click on the Pictures drop-down and select 'Online Pictures'. This will display the 'Insert Pictures' box shown as below.\n4. Enter a keyword into the Bing Image Search box and press the Enter key.\n5. Select the picture of your choice.\n20\nSuper Computers-4","6. Select the picture which you want to insert in your document.\n7. Click on 'Insert' button to insert the image.\n8. Selected images will appear in your document.\nINSERTING IMAGES USING THIS PC OPTION\nEarlier, you have added images in your document using the Online Pictures option. However, you may be required to insert your own images that you have created or saved on your computer. You can use the Pictures option to adding these images.\nSteps to add a picture :\n1. Place the cursor on the location where you want to insert the image.\n2. Click the Insert Tab.\n3. Click the Pictures option and select This PC option.\n4. An Insert Picture box appears. Select the picture from the location where it is saved on your computer.\n5. Click on the Insert button.\nThe sustainable development goals (SDGs) are a new, universal set of goals, targets and indicators that UN member states will be expected to use to frame their agendas and political policies over the next 15 years.\nINSERTING SHAPES\nYou can add shapes also to your document.\nFollow the given below steps to add a shape :\n1. To add a shape, go to the Insert tab.\n2. Click the Shapes button in the Illustrations group.\n3. Select a shape.\n4. Now simply click on the document where you want the shape to appear and drag the mouse to the work area.\nThe sustainable development goals (SDGs) are a new, universal set of goals, targets and indicators that UN member states will be expected to use to frame their agendas and political policies over the next 15 years.\n21\nSuper Computers-4","ReBOOT\nChanging the style, size and colour of text is called as text formatting.\nA font is a collection of characters in a specific style or design.\nFor a document to look attractive and presentable, different types of text should be in different sizes and colours.\nThe case describes how each letter is capitalized within a word or phrase.\nAll paragraphs usually have the same spacing between the left and right margins of a page.\nLine spacing is the blank space between the lines of a paragraph in a document.\nAlignment refers to how the text is placed in a document in relation to the margins.\nYou can set four types of alignment such as, left, right, center or justified for a paragraph in MS Word.\nBRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nDecrease Indent, Alignment, Font, Uppercase\n1. A ______ is a collection of characters in a specific style.\n2. The ______ case makes the first letter of each word capital.\n3. ________ refers to how the text is placed in a document in relation to the margins.\n4. ______ moves the paragraph towards the left margin by a fixed distance.\nB. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. Once created you cannot change the font of a text.\n2. The paragraph alignment is available in the Design tab.\n3. You can insert a picture from your document.\n4. Before applying formatting you must select the text.\n5. The Line spacing option is available in the Font group.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. It refers to how the text is placed in a document in relation to the margins.\n(i) alignment (ii) line spacing\n(iii) indentation (iv) none of these\n22\nSuper Computers-4","2. Supriya wants to add some extra space between the lines of a paragraph in her document. Which option should she use for the same?\n(i) indentation (ii) alignment\n(iii) line spacing (iv) page preview\n3. It aligns a block of text evenly between the left and right margins of the page by adding some extra spaces between words.\n(i) left align (ii) justify\n(iii) centre (iv) right align\n4. In MS Word, text is aligned to the ____________ margin by default.\n(i) left (ii) right\n(iii) centre (iv) none of these\n5. Rohit wants to move a line from typed in the document to a specific position. He will use ___________ feature of MS Word 2016.\n(i) Indenting (ii) format paint\n(iii) Line spacing (iv) paragraph\nD. Answer the following questions:\n1. Define formatting.\n2. What is font?\n3. Name the various alignment options available in Word 2016.\n23\nSuper Computers-4","4. Which options are used to insert images?\nEXECUTE TIME\n1. Sheena wants to increase the space between the lines of the text in her document. Which option in MS Word would she use for the same? (Experiential Learning)\n2. Romit has created a document on rules for being safe while using the computer. He wants to use some simple images for each point, but he cannot find them in his computer. How do you think he can arrange for these images? (Critical Thinking)\n3. Ria has created a document with several paragraphs. She notices that the paragraphs appear with a ragged right edge. What can she do to make the right edge smooth similar to the left edge? (Analytical Thinking)\nACTIVITY TIME\nLab Time Experiential Learning\nInsert the given shapes in a document and perform the following task.\n1. Insert the following shapes in MS Word.\n2. Open MS Word and perform the following tasks.\nTask 1 : Type your favourite poem. Add a title for the poem.\nTask 2 : Set the font to 'Comic Sans MS' and size to '28' of the title of the poem.\nTask 3 : Align the title of the poem to the center.\n24\nSuper Computers-4","Task 4 : Change the size to '20' and left align the text of the poem.\nTask 5 : Change the line spacing to 1.5.\n3. Create a poster in MS Word for the Plantation drive to be conducted by your school.\nUse all the features that you have learnt in MS Word to create the poster.\nTask 1 : Add a suitable slogan to the poster.\nTask 2 : Use appropriate font to make the poster attractive\nTask 3 : Align the slogan to the center of the document.\nTask 4 : Add a suitable image to your poster.\nTask 5 : Save the document and take a print of the same.\nGroup Discussion\n⊙ Discuss various formatting tools available in Word 2016.\nLet's Explore\n⊙ Take the students to the lab, show them how to resize, stretch and crop the pictures in MS Word 2016.\nFor Educators\n⊙ Demonstrate to the students the formatting in WordArt. Show them how to apply styles and WordArt to make any document attractive.\n25\nSuper Computers-4","Periodic Worksheet-1\n(Based on chapters 1 and 2)\nA. Tick (✓) the correct option :\n1. What do we call a group of 8 binary digits?\n(i) Bit (ii) Byte (iii) Nibble\n2. Which tab contains the option to change the size of the text?\n(i) Insert (ii) Home (iii) View\n3. Which of the following is a secondary storage device?\n(i) CD (ii) Pen Drive (iii) Both (i) and (ii)\n4. Which of the following is a secondary storage device?\n(i) DVD (ii) Hard Disk (iii) BluRay\nB. Fill in the blanks :\n1. ____________ devices help a computer to store the data and information.\n2. The binary digits are also known as ____________.\n3. ______________ refers to the spacing between lines in a paragraph.\n4. A _______ is a collection of characters in a special design.\nC. Write True (T) and False (F) :\n1. Memory is the ability of a computer to store and retrieve information.\n2. ROM is a type of memory that can be written and rewritten by the user.\n3. A memory card is an example of primary memory.\n4. You cannot insert pictures in a Word document.\n26\nSuper Computers-4","3\nComputer Virus & Cyber Crime\nConcept Note\nA Computer Virus is a type of malicious software, or malware that is designed to infect and spread to other computers.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nUnderstand the meaning of computer virus.\nIdentify the types of viruses.\nKnow about cybercrime.\nLearn to use Antivirus for protecting the computer from virus attacks.\nTia : Hey Niha, Do you know about computer viruses and cyber crimes?\nNiha : Yes, Tia. I have a slight idea about viruses, But I don't know much about computer viruses.\nTia : A computer virus is like a sneaky, tiny program that can get into the computer and make it behave improperly.\nNiha : Right, Tia. Tell me about cybercrime as well.\nTia : Cybercrime is when someone does something bad like stealing personal information or tricking people to give them money or any fraud.\nNiha : That's not nice at all.\nTia : Let us study more about these things to be smart and safe.\nFact Corner\nIt used to fill up the hard drive until the computer could not operate any further. This virus was created by BBN technologies in the US.\nThis first computer virus was a self-replicating virus released in 1971 called \"Creeper System\".\nINTRODUCTION\nComputer viruses have been around for quite a bit and nearly all of them have been spread\n27\nSuper Computers-4","through the internet. Most viruses have been designed to steal user’s information-processing power and/or disable the system altogether.\nThe first computer virus for MS-DOS was \"Brain\" and was released in 1986. It would overwrite the boot sector on the floppy disk and prevent the computer from booting. It was written by two brothers Basit and Amjad Farooq Alvi, who were only 17 and 24 years old at the time, from Pakistan and was originally designed as a copy protection.\nVIRUS\nA computer virus is a computer program that is written with the intention of affecting a computer. It is a small, hidden program that affects the normal functioning of your computer and enters the computer without the knowledge of the owner of the computer.\nTYPES OF VIRUSES\nVirus\nRESIDENT VIRUS\nAs the name suggests, resident viruses stay permanently in the RAM of a computer. RAM is an important part of the computer that has access to all the files. A resident virus sits in the RAM and interferes with all the instructions executed by a computer.\nSome examples of resident viruses are: MrKlunky, CMJ, Randex and Meve.\nDIRECT ACTION VIRUS\nDirect action virus is found in the main file or the root directory of the hard disk. It has been designed to become active when the computer boots itself. The agenda of this virus is to replicate itself and become active when it is executed. It waits for a specific condition to be met. Once this condition is met, it starts infecting files and directories in which it is stored.\nFor example, Rugrat.\nOVERWRITE VIRUS\nOverwrite virus is amongst the most dangerous viruses as it causes a total loss of data. If a file is infected by an overwrite virus, all the information in that file is over written by this virus. The main purpose of this virus is to delete the original information and show undecipherable words in place of the original data.\nSome examples of overwrite viruses are: Trj.Reboot, Way and Trivial.88.D.\nOverwrite Virus\n28\nSuper Computers-4","BOOT VIRUS\nBoot virus affects the booting of the computer system. This type of virus is stored in the boot sector of a floppy or hard disk. This is an important part of the computer because this is from where the computer boots itself.\nSome examples of boot viruses are: Form and Michelangelo.\nFact\nCorner\nThe term 'Computer Virus' was formally defined by Fred Cohen.\nMACRO VIRUS\nThe macro virus affects the files that contain macros. Macros are kinds of mini-programs that help the user by automating a series of operations so that they may be performed as a single action.\nSome examples of macro viruses are: O97M/Y2K, Relax, Melissa.A and Bablas.\nMacro Virus\nDIRECTORY VIRUS\nIt infects files and changes the path addresses of files. This virus removes the original file and places itself in its position. Whenever you access that file, the computer automatically runs the virus.\nDirectory Virus\nPOLYMORPHIC VIRUS\nIt replicates and makes a large number of copies of itself. Every time it infects a system. It encodes itself in a different way. This makes it impossible for anti-virus programs to locate it.\nSome examples of polymorphic viruses are: Satan Bug, Elkern, Tuareg and Marburg.\nPolymorphic Virus\nFILE INFECTOR\nIt infects all .exe and .com files. When you run a .exe or a .com infected file, the virus is also executed. Some examples of file infectors are: Vienna, Jerusalem, Dark Avenger and Raindrop.\nFile Infector\n29\nSuper Computers-4","WORM\nIt is a program that is designed to slow down the operating speed of the computer. It can self-replicate very fast. Its main purpose is to affect the speed of the computer and not the data of the computer. Some examples of worms are: Mapson, Lovgate.F, PSWBugbear.B, Trile. C and Sobig.D.\nWorm\nTROJANS OR TROJAN HORSES\nThey enter through removable data storage devices. Their main purpose is not to replicate themselves but to affect the data stored on the computer.\nLOGIC BOMBS\nThey are designed in such a way that they get activated only when certain conditions are met. Like Trojans, these also do not replicate themselves. Logic bombs cannot be detected easily as they are encrypted or camouflaged segments of other important programs that you keep running on your system.\nLogic Bomb\nTime Bomb\nTIME BOMBS\nThey are designed in such a way that they activate themselves at a time when certain conditions are met.\nSYMPTOMS OF VIRUS INFECTION\nHow will you come to know that your computer has been affected? Given below is a list of certain symptoms that you will see when a computer gets infected.\nThe operating speed of the computer gets greatly reduced.\nInstead of the original files, you see undecipherable characters in your file.\nSymptoms of Virus Infection\nThe last modified date changes. The computer records show the last time a file is worked upon. If a virus affects a file, the computer changes the last modified date of that file. Checking the last modified date of the file can also reveal if the file is infected or not.\nThe computer hangs itself and stops working at all.\nThe floppy disk is accessed without any reason.\n30\nSuper Computers-4","The screen suddenly gets covered with odd pictures and graphics like falling letters or bouncing balls.\nFile size increases on its own.\nStrange characters start appearing in the name of the infected file.\nUndecipherable messages start appearing on the screen.\nApart from the symptoms given above, if your computer starts behaving in a strange or abnormal manner then it means that the computer has been affected by a virus.\nPROTECTION FROM VIRUS INFECTIONS\nIn order to prevent viruses from attacking your computer, you can take the following steps:\nMake backup copies of all the valuable and important data that you have. Update these copies regularly so that, at any point of time, you may have the latest backup copy.\nDo not start your computer with a floppy disk in the floppy drive.\nDo not use pirated software, especially games.\nDo not visit sites that have not been authorized.\nProtecting Computer from Virus\nDo not load any program through any untrustworthy disc.\nBe very careful of the person you allow to use your computer.\nInstall an anti-virus in your computer and update it regularly.\nCYBER/COMPUTER CRIME\nAnother very big problem that the world is facing today is that of cyber/computer crime. Cyber crimes refer to the crimes that are conducted over the Internet. Computer crimes refer to the crimes that are conducted on an individual stand-alone computer with the intention of harming somebody. In recent years, both these crimes have increased manifold. These crimes are causing great loss to the society and to individuals.\nCyber crimes are committed in various ways and have different names.\nLet us study about some of these crimes and their names:\nHACKING\nHacking refers to the process of breaking into a computer with the intention of stealing information from that computer. A person who hacks a computer is called a hacker.\nHacking\n31\nSuper Computers-4","CRACKING\nCracking refers to the process of cracking into a computer with the purpose of stealing the passwords of the computer, files or programs.\nMALWARE & ANTIVIRUS\nMalware is unwanted software that is developed to damage & destroy computer systems.\nExamples are viruses, worms, Trojan viruses, etc.\nMalware & Antivirus\nAntivirus is a type of software that is used to prevent, scan, detect and delete viruses from a computer. There are various types of antivirus softwares such as Norton, McAfee, AVG, Avira, etc.\nReBOOT\n© Computer virus affects the normal functioning of your computer.\n© There are different types of computer viruses, namely Resident Virus, Direct Action Virus, Overwrite Viruses, Boot Viruses, Macro Viruses, etc.\n© Cyber Crimes refer to the crimes that are conducted over the Internet.\n© Hacking means breaking into a computer with the intention of stealing information from that computer.\n© Cracking means stealing the password of a computer.\nBRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nResident Floppy Macro Virus Direct Action Computer Virus\n1. ______________ affects the files that contain macros.\n2. Boot Virus is stored in the boot sector of a ______________.\n3. ______________ virus is found in the main file of the hard disk.\n4. ______________ can affect the normal functioning of a computer.\n5. ______________ virus stays permanently in the RAM of a computer.\n32\nSuper Computers-4","B. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. File infector affects all .exe and .com files.\n2. The polymorphic virus does not replicate itself.\n3. Trojans enter through removable data storage devices.\n4. Directory virus infects files and changes the path addresses of files.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. Which of the following viruses is a type of CMJ?\n(i) Boot Virus (ii) Resident Virus\n(iii) Macro Virus (iv) None of these\n2. Which of the following are encrypted segments of other important programs?\n(i) Time Bombs (ii) Logical Bombs\n(iii) Worms (iv) None of these\n3. Which of the following refers to the crimes that are conducted over the Internet?\n(i) Hacking (ii) Cyber Crime\n(iii) Cracking (iv) None of these\n4. Which of the following means using logic to guess the password?\n(i) Cracking (ii) Hacking\n(iii) Virus Programming (iv) None of these\nD. Answer the following questions:\n1. What are the symptoms of virus infection?\n2. Differentiate between hacking and cracking.\n33\nSuper Computers-4","3. Define overwrite virus and polymorphic virus.\n4. What is a computer virus? How can it harm your computer?\nACTIVITY TIME\n⊙ Collect information related to antivirus and make a project file on it.\nGroup Discussion\n⊙ Discuss the types of computer viruses and their symptoms.\nLet's Explore\n⊙ Explore any software that can kill the virus in a computer.\nFor Educators\n⊙ Make the students familiar with the various points to be kept in mind while using the computer and the Internet.\n⊙ Demonstrate how a computer virus affects the functioning of a computer.\n34\nSuper Computers-4","4\nPowerPoint 2016:\nPresentation\nConcept Note\nA presentation is like a show-and-tell where someone talks about a topic using slides or pictures. It helps people understand and learn about something.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nUnderstand the need for presentation software called PowerPoint.\nIdentify the components of PowerPoint Window.\nCreate and add slides.\nNiha : Tia, Have you heard about PowerPoint 2016?\nTia : No, Niha. What is it?\nNiha : It's a special computer program for creating slides with pictures and words. We can use it to make our ideas look amazing and share them with others.\nTia : That sounds wonderful, Niha! Tell me more!\nNiha : Sure, Tia. Let's dive into the world of PowerPoint 2016!\nMS-PowerPoint is a presentation software that allows you to create interesting and exciting presentations in the form of slides. It is a part of Microsoft Office package and it is widely used in schools, offices and many other places.\n35\nSuper Computers-4","STARTING POWERPOINT 2016\nTo start PowerPoint, follow the given steps :\nStep 3: Select the Microsoft Office option from the sub-menu.\nStep 1: Go to Start button.\nStep 4: Select the Microsoft PowerPoint 2016 option.\nStep 2: Go to All Programs.\nStarting PowerPoint\nQuick Access Toolbar\nTitle Bar\nTabs\nRibbon\nGroup\nSlides\nMongibello Artisan Pasta\nStatus Bar\nMS PowerPoint Window\nCOMPONENTS OF THE POWERPOINT WINDOW\nA. File Menu and Backstage View\nB. Quick Access Toolbar\nC. Ribbon\nD. Slides Pane\nE. Slide Area\nF. Task Pane\nClick to add title\nComponents of PowerPoint\nInfo\nBackstage View\nA. FILE MENU AND BACKSTAGE VIEW\nWhen you click on the File menu, you see the Backstage view that contains all the options to create, open, save and print the presentations.\n36\nSuper Computers-4","B. QUICK ACCESS TOOLBAR\nIt is a customizable toolbar placed by default above the Ribbon. Here you can add icons for your often-used commands. It can also be placed below the Ribbon.\nQuick Access Toolbar\nC. RIBBON\nThe Ribbon contains groups of buttons for various options; such as cut, copy, paste, find and replace.\nRibbon\nD. SLIDES PANE\nThe slides pane is located on the left side of the interface. It shows thumbnails of all the slides in the open presentation.\nSlides Pane\nE. SLIDE AREA\nIt displays the active slide.\nSlide Area\n37\nSuper Computers-4","F. TASK PANE\nThe Task Pane contains more options and appears when you choose an option in one of the Ribbon tabs. For example, if you click on the Format Background button within the Design tab of the Ribbon, the Format Background task pane opens\nG. STATUS BAR\nA horizontal strip at the bottom of window provides information about the opened presentation, like slide number, applied Theme, etc. It also includes the view and zoom options.\nTask Pane\nStatus Bar\nCREATING A PRESENTATION USING A TEMPLATE\nWe can use the readily available themes or templates to make a presentation more attractive. This allows us to save time in creating our own template.\nSteps to select a theme or template for the presentation are as follows :\n1. Click on File and select New.\n2. Type a theme name in the search box.\n3. Click on the search icon.\n4. Select the required template from the search result.\n38\nSuper Computers-4","4. Click on the Create option. A new presentation is created. It is based on the selected template.\nSELECTING A SLIDE LAYOUT\nIn PowerPoint, a slide layout is used to define the placement of slide elements such as title and content. When you create a new presentation, it has a single slide with the 'Title Slide' layout.\nThe dotted rectangular boxes are called the Placeholders. They are used to show the placement of various objects on the slide.\nWhenever you add a new slide to a presentation, its layout is selected as 'Title and Content' by default. You can, however, change the layout of this slide.\nSteps to select a slide layout :\n1. Select the Home tab.\n2. Click on the Layout option from the slides group.\n3. Click on the desired layout from the list.\n39\nSuper Computers-4","The slide layout of your slide changes to the selected layout.\nClick to add title\nINSERTING A NEW SLIDE\nYou can add multiple slides to a presentation. On each slide, you can place different objects and add animation effects to all the objects on the slides.\nSteps to add a new slide :\n1. Click on the Home tab.\n2. Click on the drop-down arrow next to the New Slide command to see all the available slide layouts.\n3. Select the desired layout.\nFile Home Insert Design Transitions Animations Slide Show Review\nCut\nCopy\nPaste\nFormat Painter\nClipboard\nNew Slide\nLayout\nReset\nSection\nOffice Theme\nTitle Slide\nTitle and Content\nSection Header\nTitle and Content\nTwo Content\nComparison\nTitle Only\nBlank\nContent with Caption\nPicture with Caption\nDuplicate Selected Slides\nSlides from Outline...\nReuse Slides...\nActivity-1\n1. Create a new PowerPoint presentation, with the theme 'Sports'.\n2. Add a new slide to this presentation.\n3. Change the slide layout of this slide to 'Two Content' styles.\n40\nSuper Computers-4","ADDING TEXT TO A SLIDE\nMS PowerPoint allows you to add text to a slide. You can add the title, subtitle and content of the slide using the placeholders.\nSteps to add text to a slide :\n1. Click inside the placeholder.\nClick to add subtitle\nMy Favourite Sport\nClick to add subtitle\n2. Type the desired text.\n3. Using the sizing handle, adjust the size of the placeholder.\n4. You can reposition the placeholder by dragging it to the desired location.\nMy Favourite Sport\nClick to add subtitle\n41\nSuper Computers-4","ADDING IMAGES TO A SLIDE\nYou can add images to a slide to make your presentation more attractive. PowerPoint allows you to add images that are saved on the computer.\nSteps to add images from the local folder of a computer :\n1. Click on the Insert menu.\n2. Click on the Pictures option. You can either chose This PC option or online pictures option from the appearing list. Here we have selected This PC option.\n3. From the Insert picture box, Select the folder where the image is stored.\n4. Select the image to be inserted.\n5. Click on the Insert button.\nActivity-2\n1. Create a PowerPoint presentation on the topic \"Natural things and Man-made things\".\n2. Add suitable images to the slide.\nAPPLYING THEMES\nYou have learned to create a presentation using a template. You can also apply a theme to your slides after creating them. These themes can be found in the Design tab.\nA theme is a pre-defined set of font, colours and visual effects that can be applied throughout the presentation. They are used to make the presentation look more appealing and professional. They are also used to make your presentation look consistent.\nSteps to apply a theme to a slide :\n1. Click on the Design tab.\n2. Click on the drop-down arrow to see the all available themes.\n42\nSuper Computers-4","4. Select the desired theme.\n5. The selected theme will be applied to the entire presentation.\nVIEWING A SLIDE SHOW\nPowerPoint provides the option of presenting all the slides of a presentation in the form of a slide show. The slide show displays all slides in a presentation, one by one, in full screen. You can view all animation and transition effects used in the presentation in a slide show. You can use the arrow keys, space bar, and backspace keys to move from one slide to another. You can also right-click during a slide show to go to the next, previous or last viewed slide.\nSteps to view a slide show:\n1. Click on the Slide Show tab.\n2. Select ok From Beginning option.\nFact Corner\nThe Esc key is used to stop the slide show.\nTech Corner\nTo run a slide show from the beginning, press the F5 button on the keyboard.\nTo run a slideshow from the current slide, use the Shift + F5 button on the keyboard.\nSAVING A PRESENTATION\nTo save a presentation, follow the given steps:\n1. Click on the File tab.\n2. Click Save As option.\n3. On the left-hand pane, in the Save As dialog box; select the folder, where you want to save your presentation.\n43\nSuper Computers-4","4. In the File name box, type the name for the presentation or accept the default file name.\nFact Corner\nPowerPoint presentations are saved with pptx extension.\n5. Click on Save.\nEXIT POWERPOINT\nTo exit from PowerPoint, follow the given steps :\n1. Click on the File tab and choose the Close option. The Presentation will be closed.\n2. If you have made changes in the presentation since it was last saved, a dialog box will appear with three buttons— Save, Don't Save and Cancel.\n3. Click on the appropriate option.\nREBOOT\nMS PowerPoint is a presentation software that allows you to create presentations in the form of slides.\nMS PowerPoint window has various components, such as Ribbon, Quick Access Toolbar, Slides tab, Title bar, etc.\nIn MS-PowerPoint, you can create and save presentations.\nIn a PowerPoint, a slide layout is used to define the placement of slide elements such as title and content.\nA theme is a pre-defined set of font, colours and visual effects that can be applied throughout the presentation.\nYou can add the title, subtitle and content to the slide using the placeholders.\nBRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nLayout Presentation Tabs Save As\n1. MS-PowerPoint is a ____________ software.\n2. Click on ____________ option to save a presentation.\n3. Ribbon consists ____________ , Groups and Commands.\n4. ____________ is used to define the placement of slide elements.\n44\nSuper Computers-4","B. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. MS PowerPoint is present in MS-Office option.\n2. Status bar is located at the bottom of the screen.\n3. You cannot change the layout of the slide.\n4. The dotted rectangular boxes are called placeholders.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. It displays thumbnail versions of slides in a presentation.\n(i) Ribbon (ii) Slide Pane\n(iii) Slides Tab (iv) None of the above\n2. Which of the following options allows you to view the slide show from the beginning?\n(i) From Beginning (ii) From Middle\n(iii) From Current (iv) None of he above\n3. Which of the following is an extension of PowerPoint presentation?\n(i) .pptx (ii) .docx\n(iii) .xls (iv) None\n4. Which of the following contains colour schemes, fonts and visual effects for presentations?\n(i) Theme (ii) Layout\n(iii) Design (iv) None\nD. Answer the following questions.\n1. Give three uses of PowerPoint.\n2. How can you start a slide show in MS PowerPoint?\n3. What are the steps to open PowerPoint?\n45\nSuper Computers-4","4. How can you save a presentation in MS PowerPoint?\nEXECUTE TIME (Art Integration)\n1. Saira has been given the assignment to present her views on Positive thinking. Which software should she use?\n2. Sujan is making a presentation and wants to add two more slides. Can you help him in his task?\nACTIVITY TIME\nDefine the following : Subject Enrichment\n(a) Quick Access toolbar\n(b) Ribbon\n(c) Slides tab\n(d) Title bar\nLab Time Analytical Thinking\nCreate a presentation of five slides on \" Chandrayaan 3\" in PowerPoint.\nCreate a presentation on \"My School\" containing 10 slides.\nGroup Discussion\nExplore how you can insert images in a presentation.\nLet's Explore\nDiscuss the uses of MS PowerPoint 2016.\nFor Educators\nShow the students some PowerPoint presentations highlighting different elements like slides, text, and images and familiarize students with the PowerPoint interface.\n46\nSuper Computers-4","Test Paper-1\n(Based on chapters 1 to 4)\nA. Fill in the blanks :\n1. It we delete the file or folder, it moves to _______________.\n2. You can view and organize files using _______________ application.\n3. The data which we put into the computer is processed by _______________.\n4. The _____________ Virus affects the booting of a computer system.\nB. Write True (T) and False (F) :\n1. MS windows is not an operating system.\n2. RAM stands for Random Accept Memory.\n3. A folder is a place to store one or more files.\n4. You cannot change the layout of a slide once a presentation is created.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer :\n1. Which storage device has the highest storage capacity?\n(i) Pen Drive (ii) CD (iii) Hard Drive\n2. This PC is the name given to the _____________ icon.\n(i) Desktop (ii) My Computer (iii) Gallery\n3. An _______________ is used to handle the viruses in a computer.\n(i) Antivirus (ii) Hacker (iii) Boot Recorder\n4. Each file in a folder has a name that is _______________.\n(i) Unique (ii) Multiple (iii) Single\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. Define Memory.\n2. Describe RAM & ROM.\n3. Differentiate between file and folder.\n4 . Differentiate internal memory & external memory.\n47\nSuper Computers-4","5\nGUI Operating System–\nDesktop Management\nConcept Note\nAn operating system is a program that is first loaded onto your computer system when you open it.\nIt manages the computer hardware and all other applications and programs in a computer.\nLearning OUTCOMES\n■ Know about Windows 10.\n■ Learn features of Windows 10.\n■ Learn to change the wallpaper.\n■ Learn to manage multiple windows.\nNiha : Hey Tia, Do you know what an operating system is?\nTia : No, Niha, what is it?\nNiha : An operating system is a special program for our computer. It helps our computer to run smoothly, and Windows 10 is one of them.\nTia : I get it, Niha. So, what can Windows 10 do?\nNiha : Well, Tia, it performs all the basic tasks like file management, memory management, process management, handling input and output devices, etc.\nTia : Great Niha! I am eager to learn more about it.\nThe currently most widely used version of the Microsoft Windows operating system is Windows 10, which was released on July 29, 2015. The first GUI version of Windows was introduced in 1995 and was called Windows 95. Windows has several distinct versions over the years, including Windows 8, Windows 7, Windows Vista (2006), and Windows XP, Windows 98, Windows NT. Windows 11 is the most recent version of Windows operating system released on October 5, 2021.\n48\nSuper Computers-4","While previous versions of Windows were mainly designed to run on desktop and laptop computers, Windows 10 is also designed to run on tablets.\nAn operating system is a system software that acts as an interface between the user and the hardware. An operating system manages all of the hardware and software on a computer. Without it, the computer won't be able to work. It keeps track of information regarding creation, deletion, copy, and storage of files.\nFEATURES OF WINDOWS 10\nWindows 10 offers various new features that make it user-friendly and easy to use as compared to its previous versions.\nFollowing are some of the important features of Windows 10:\nWindows 10 has reintroduced a more traditional Start menu. In this, it has updated graphics and you can turn to full screen optionally.\nCortana is a new feature of Windows 10. It is a virtual assistant just like Siri, Google Voice assistant, and Alexa. You can use it to set reminders, get weather forecasts, send an email, find files, search the Internet, and so on.\nIt is bundled with a new and improved web browser named Microsoft Edge. It is faster, more secure, and with many new features as compared to its previous browser Internet Explorer.\nWindows 10 offers a feature of multiple virtual desktops and an easy task view to switch between tabs and avoid clustering of multiple windows.\nFact Corner\nYou can access multiple windows with the use of (Window + Tab) button.\nWindows 10 allows users to switch between tablet mode and desktop mode.\nThe Microsoft Store provides a bundle of updated apps including videos, music, movies, photos, maps, messaging, mail, calendar, etc.\nSnap Assist is a new feature that helps users reorganize screen space to make the best use of it.\nTech Corner\nWhen you snap one of the opened windows on the screen, Snap Assist displays the thumbnails of the other opened apps that can be snapped in the unused space. You can use both windows simultaneously.\n49\nSuper Computers-4","BASIC COMPONENTS OF WINDOWS DESKTOP\nThe Windows Desktop is the main screen. Here, you can access the Start Menu, Taskbar, and other icons. Windows 10 also introduces a search box in the Taskbar, from where you can browse both your computer and the Web.\nSTART\nSEARCH\nTASKBAR\nNOTIFICATION AREA\nTech Corner\nThe small pictures or graphics on the desktop represent an application or programs are called icons.\nSTART MENU\nThis is located at the bottom left corner of Windows 10. This is the place where you can search for any application. When you click on the Start button at the bottom left of the screen, you get two panels, side by side. The left column displays the pinned, recently added, and the most-used apps. The right column displays live tiles that you can customize, resize and reorganize.\n50\nSuper Computers-4","Fact Corner\nLive Tiles display updated information instead of the app icon. For example, the Weather tile shows the current weather for your default location; the Mail tile displays recent email messages; and the Calendar tile shows your upcoming events.\nTASKBAR\nThis strip along the bottom of the screen displays icons for each running app. It displays the Start button on the extreme left side. Next to the Start button is the Search box that facilitates browsing through both your computer and the web. To the right of the Search box is the Task View button.\nThe Taskbar holds the notification area on the right side, which includes a clock and a group of other icons. These icons display the status and notifications about the programs running on a computer, e.g., network connectivity, battery status, volume, OneDrive, etc.\nStart Button\nTask View Button\nPeek Button\nSearch Box\nWindows 10 Taskbar\nNotification Area\nICONS\nIcons are small pictures that represent files, folders, and apps. They play a very important role in graphical user interface (GUI).\nLet us learn about some of the commonly used icons :\nThis PC\nIt gives you a view of the available drives and their content stored on the computer system. Double-clicking on any drive's icon will display the files and folders in that drive.\nApplication Icons\nThese are the graphical images that give a quick access to the related application.\nShortcut Icons\nA shortcut is an icon containing a direct route to a specific application, document, or a folder. It has a small jump arrow on its lower-left corner.\nDocument Icons\nThe active document window of an application can be minimized to a document icon, which gets placed on the taskbar. You can restore the document window by clicking on the Document icon again.\nNetwork\nIt allows you to access the shared files or devices on different computers in a network.\nRecycle Bin\nThe Recycle Bin is a folder that holds the deleted files/folders. These deleted files/folders can either be restored to the original location or permanently deleted from the system.\n51\nSuper Computers-4","LAUNCHING AN APPLICATION\nTo launch an application from the Start menu, perform the following steps :\nStep 1: Click the Start button on the taskbar. The Start menu appears.\nStep 2: Click the All Apps button present at the bottom of the Start menu. A list of all apps stored on the computer appears in alphabetical order.\nStep 3: Scroll to find the required application and click on it. The application window appears on the screen and its icon is displayed on the taskbar.\nPINNING PROGRAMS TO THE START MENU\nPinning a program to the Start menu creates a shortcut for an application. This shortcut is in the form of a tile in the tile list to the right of the Start menu. Using this shortcut, you can open the application easily with a single click. You do not need to search for it or scroll through the All Apps list.\nSteps to pin a program to the Start menu :\nStep 1: Select Start → All apps. A list of all applications is displayed on the screen.\nStep 2: Select the application you wish to pin from the list and right-click on it. A menu appears.\nStep 3: Select the Pin to Start option from the menu. The selected application will be pinned to the Start menu.\nActivity-1\nPin the MS Paint and Word 2016 to the Start Menu.\nUNPIN A PROGRAM\nTo unpin a program, right-click on the program in the Start menu and select the Unpin from Start option from the menu.\n52\nSuper Computers-4","Tech Corner\nYou can also pin a program as a tile by dragging and dropping it to the tile section of the Start menu.\nRESIZING TILES\nYou can also resize the tiles according to your choice. Tiles are generally available in four different sizes: Small, medium, wide, and large. To resize a tile follow the given steps :\n1. Right-click on the tile and select the option Resize.\n2. Select the size of your choice.\nActivity-2\nExplore all the options of resizing and resize all the live tiles to medium size.\nFind out the names of at least 10 applications available on your computer by using the 'All Programs' option in the Start menu.\nDISPLAYING MULTIPLE WINDOWS AT A TIME\nIn Windows 10, you can display multiple applications side by side.\nSteps to open multiple windows at the same time :\n1. First, open Word 2016 and PowerPoint 2016.\n2. Now, right-click any-where on the blank portion of the taskbar.\n3. Click on the Show windows side by side-option from the Shortcut menu. Both the application windows will be displayed, side by side.\nCUSTOMIZING DESKTOP\nWindows 10 makes it easy to customize the look and feel of your desktop.\nSteps to customize the desktop :\n1. Right-click anywhere on the desktop.\n53\nSuper Computers-4","2. Select Personalize from the drop-down menu. The Settings will appear.\nFrom here, you can change the font, colors scheme, themes and background of your computer.\nCHANGING DESKTOP BACKGROUND\nYou can change the desktop background to change the appearance of your desktop. The background image that appears on your desktop is called wallpaper.\nSteps to change the desktop background :\n1. Right click on the desktop and select Personalize.\n2. Click on Background. Then click on the drop-down arrow of the Background.\n3. Select the picture that you want to make the wallpaper.\n4. You can select the size of the picture from the drop-down menu of the Choose a Fit option.\nTech Corner\nYou can also choose the Browse option to select the picture stored on your computer.\nCHANGING THE COLOR SCHEME\nSteps to change the colour of your desktop :\n1. Right-click on the desktop and select Personalize.\n2. Click on Colors.\n3. Scroll down to the color palette to choose any colour.\n4. The selected colour will be applied.\n54\nSuper Computers-4","Settings\nHome\nThemes\nCurrent theme: Custom\nBackground\nHarmony\nColor\nOrchid light\nSounds\nWindows Default\nMouse cursor\nWindows Default\nSave theme\nChange theme\nGet more themes in Microsoft Store\nWindows\n1 images\nWindows (light)\n1 images\nWindows 10\n5 images\nFlowers\n6 images\nFind a setting\nPersonalization\nBackground\nColors\nLock screen\nThemes\nFonts\nStart\nTaskbar\nCHANGING THE THEME\nThe steps to change the windows theme are as follows :\n1. Right-click on the desktop and select Personalize.\n2. Click on Themes.\n3. Scroll down and select the theme that you want to use.\n4. The selected theme will be applied.\nActivity-3\nChange the desktop, color scheme and theme of your computer to make it attractive as per your choice.\nReBOOT\nThe first GUI version of Windows was introduced in 1995 and was called Windows 95.\nCortana is a new feature of Windows 10. It is a virtual assistant just like Siri, Google, and Alexa, provided by Microsoft.\nWindows 10 offers a feature of multiple virtual desktops and an easy task view to switch between tabs.\nSnap Assist is a new feature that helps users to reorganize screen space to make the best use of it.\nSearch box allows browsing through both your computer and the web.\nIcons are small pictures that represent files, folders, and apps.\n55\nSuper Computers-4","BRAIN BOOSTER A. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box. Operating System, Interface, Start, Taskbar 1. An operating system provides an ________ between the user and the hardware. 2. A computer cannot work without an _____________ 3. The horizontal bar located at the bottom of the desktop is called ________. 4. The __________ menu is the starting point to view all the applications stored on your computer. B. Write true (T) or false (F). 1. The desktop is the main screen of a computer. 2. An icon represents a file, folder, or program. 3. The GUI lets you use the mouse to click icons, buttons, and menus. 4. The Start button allows the user to access the installed applications. C. Tick (✓) the right answer. 1. Which of the following is the latest Windows operating system? (i) Windows 7 (ii) Windows 8 (iii) Windows 10 (iv) Windows XP 2. Which of the following holds the clock and other options on the desktop? (i) Icon (ii) Taskbar (iii) Wallpaper (iv) Start 3. Which option do you click to change the desktop background? (i) Personalize (ii) Desktop (iii) Wallpaper (iv) Shortcut 4. The first screen of a computer is called the ________. (i) shortcut (ii) folder (iii) desktop (iv) taskbar 56 Super Computers-4","D. Answer the following questions.\nThe following questions text.\n1. Write any four features of Windows 10.\n2. Write the steps to change the theme of a computer system.\n3. Explain any four components of a desktop\n4. What is the use of the search box on the taskbar?\nEXECUTE TIME (Image Based)\n1. Observe the following picture and Label the following given parts:\n(i) Icons\n(ii) Notification Area\n(iii) Start Button\n(iv) Wallpaper\n57\nSuper Computers-4","Application Based\n2. Seema wants to see the weather forecast. Which feature of Windows 10 helps to check the weather forecast information?\nACTIVITY TIME\nDescribe the following:\na. Taskbar :\nb. Desktop :\nc. Recycle Bin :\nd. Icon :\nLab Time Experiential Learning\nIn the presence of your teacher/parent change the desktop background using a variety of pictures and styles as follows:\na. Picture stretched on the desktop\nb. Picture only at the centre of the desktop.\nc. Picture tiles on the desktop.\nGroup Discussion\nDiscuss various features of Windows 10.\nLet's Explore\nExplore what is Graphical User Interface or GUI.\nFor Educators\nDemonstrate to the students how to change the desktop properties.\n58\nSuper Computers-4","6\nIntroduction to Spreadsheet\nConcept Note\nA spreadsheet is a computer program that helps us to organize, store and analyze data in tabular form.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nSpreadsheet Program.\nStarting Excel.\nWorkbook and Worksheet.\nComponents of a Workbook.\nMoving around the Spreadsheet.\nEntering of Data.\nPerforming Calculations.\nSaving a Workbook.\nOpening a Saved Workbook.\nTia : Hi Niha! Today we are going to learn about MS Excel.\nNiha : Hey, Tia! I'm not sure. Is it something fun?\nTia : Well, it's actually a computer program that helps you work with numbers and data. You can use it to make lists, calculate things and create charts.\nNiha : Oh, that sounds interesting! Can you show me how to use it?\nTia : Of course, Niha. I'd love to help you get started with MS Excel!.\nSPREADSHEET PROGRAM\nA spreadsheet is an application program used to store data in rows and columns, i.e. tabular form. It can also be used to sort, calculate and manipulate data. Examples of spreadsheet applications are Google Sheets, LibreOffice Calc, Microsoft Excel and OpenOffice.\n59\nSuper Computers-4","You can enter, edit and save data in a spreadsheet application. You can also present the data\nin a graphical form using a spreadsheet application.\nNow, in this chapter, You will learn about MS Excel. Following is a window of MS Excel.\nLike other spreadsheet applications, MS Excel also contains columns and rows. Columns are arranged vertically. Rows are arranged horizontally. The rows and columns cross each other to form boxes called cells. Data is entered in these cells.\nSTARTING EXCEL\nFollow the given steps to create a worksheet in MS Excel :\n1. Type 'Excel' in the search bar.\n2. Select the Microsoft Excel option.\n3. After selecting MS Excel, a new Spreadsheet file appears.\nMS EXCEL WINDOW\nThe columns in Excel are named using letters such as A, B, C, D. Rows are named using numbers such as 1, 2, 3. A cell is named by its column name followed by its row name. For example, the name of the first cell is A1.\nName box\nInput line\nA2 fx Car\nA B C\nName of Toy Price\nCar 20\nBubble gun 50\nPuzzle 60\nBoard game 40\nCells\nColumns\nRow headings\nThe Name box displays the name of the active cell and the Input line shows the content. It can be used for editing the content of the cell. The mouse pointer appears as a plus sign in Excel.\n60\nSuper Computer-4","WORKBOOK AND WORKSHEET\nWORKBOOK\nWhen you start MS Excel, a new spreadsheet is created. This spreadsheet can be saved on your computer as a file. This file is called a workbook. The workbook is given the name 'Book1' by default. You can change this name while saving it to make it meaningful.\nWorkbook\nWORKSHEET\nEach workbook can have more than one spreadsheet. Each of these is called a worksheet.\nWorksheet\nClick on the New sheet button to add another worksheet\nCOMPONENTS OF A WORKBOOK\nFollowing are the main components of a workbook :\nMENU BAR\nIt consists of menus with a drop-down list of submenus. It gives access to basic commands such as File, Save and so on.\nMenu bar\nFORMULA TOOLBAR\nThis toolbar contains the Name Box and the Input Line textbox. The Name Box displays the name of the active cell and the Input Line displays its content. It can be used to edit the content of a cell. In case of a calculated value, it displays the formula used for the calculation.\nFormula toolbar\n61\nSuper Computers-4","COLUMNS AND ROWS\nYou know that a spreadsheet is in the form of a grid or a table that organizes data into rows and columns.\nColumns : They are arranged vertically. Each column is identified by a column heading. Columns are named with letters A, B, C, etc.\nRows : They are arranged horizontally. A row number identifies each row. Row numbers start with 1,2,3, etc.\nCell: The intersection of a column and a row forms a cell. You enter data in a cell. Each cell also has a name. This is formed by its column name followed by its row name. For example, the first cell in the upper left corner is A1 (column A, row 1).\nENTERING OF DATA\nYou can enter text or number data in a spreadsheet. These are also called values. Some text values are used to describe the data in a column or row. They are called spreadsheet labels.\nFollow the given steps to enter data :\n1. Click on cell A1.\n2. Type the label 'Colours'. Press Enter.\nSimilarly, you can enter number data in a worksheet.\nActivity-1\n1. Open the spreadsheet software.\n2. Type the names of the items in your Pencil box into Column A of your spreadsheet.\n3. Count how many items you have and enter it in Column B.\nPERFORMING CALCULATIONS\nYou can perform calculations on number data in Excel using formulas and using commands.\nUSING FORMULA\nIn Excel, every formula begins with an 'equals' (=) sign. For example, to add two values the formula will be =A1+B1.\nLet's learn to create a formula to add three numbers.\n62\nSuper Computers-4","Follow the given steps to add three numbers :\n1. Open the Excel worksheet.\n2. Enter the values in A1, B1 and C1 as shown and press Enter.\n3. Now, in the input line type the name of cells whose values need to be added.\nFor example: A1+B1+C1\n4. Press Enter. Now you can see the sum of A1, B1 and C1 in D1.\nSAVING A SPREADSHEET\nWhen you create a spreadsheet file, you need to save it on your computer. Once you have saved your file, you can open it any time for make changes in it.\nSteps to save your spreadsheet :\nFact Corner\n1. Click the 'Save' button.\n2. A 'Save As' dialog box appears. Select 'The Desktop' location from here.\n3. Give a name to the file.\n4. Click 'Save'.\nThe Save option allows you to save a document in the same location with the same name.\nThe Save As option is used to save a document with a different name and/or a different location.\nOPENING A SAVED SPREADSHEET\nYou can work with spreadsheet files that are already saved on your computer. You can reopen the files and then make changes in them.\nFollow the given steps to open a spreadsheet :\n1. Click the 'File' option.\n2. Click Open.\n3. Select the spreadsheet that you have saved under Recent Workbooks.\nReBoot\nA Spreadsheet is an application program used to store data in rows and columns, i.e. tabular form.\nYou can enter, edit and save data in a spreadsheet application. You can also present the data in a graphical form using a spreadsheet application.\nYou can enter text or number data in a spreadsheet.\nExcel can also be used to sort, calculate and manipulate data.\nEach workbook can have more than one spreadsheet. Each of these is called a worksheet.\nYou can perform calculations on number data in Excel using formulas and commands.\nIn Excel, every formula begins with an 'equals' (=) sign.\n63\nSuper Computers-4","BRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nequal to,    text or letters,    formulas,    workbook\n1. You can enter _________ or _____________ data in a spreadsheet.\n2. The ___________ command allows you to calculate values.\n3. A ____________ can contain many worksheets.\n4. In Excel, every formula begins with an _________ sign.\nB. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. Excel is a spreadsheet program.\n2. A workbook can contain many worksheets.\n3. You cannot enter characters in a cell.\n4. You can do only addition in Excel.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. Which of the following is not a column heading?\n(i) A0    (ii) A\n(iii) F    (iv) D\n2. Which key is used to move to the next cell in the same column of a worksheet?\n(i) Tab    (ii) Enter\n(iii) Space    (iv) Shift\n3. A workbook contains _______________.\n(i) Worksheets    (ii) Spread sheets\n(iii) Both (i) and (ii)    (iv) None of these\n4. Which of the following is a spreadsheet program?\n(i) Calc    (ii) Excel\n(iii) Google Sheets    (iv) All of the above\nD. Answer the following questions.\n1. What is a spreadsheet?\n64\nSuper Computers-4","2. Name any three spreadsheet programs.\n3. Write the steps to find the sum of values using a formula.\n4. Define the following: Workbook, Worksheet\nEXECUTE TIME (Life Skills)\n1. Reena wants to calculate the total amount spent on shopping. What will be the formula?\n2. Sandhya wants to create two worksheets in a workbook. But she does not know how to do the same. Can you help her?\nACTIVITY TIME\nLab Time\nCreate the following worksheet that shows the toys sold in a day.\na. The number of toys remaining for each type.\nb. The total number of toys you had\nc. The total number of toys sold.\nGroup Discussion\nDiscuss the various uses of Microsoft Excel 2016.\nLet's Explore\nUse Microsoft Excel 2016 to create a basic shopping list with items and their prices.\nFor Educators\nDemonstrate to students how to perform calculations using formulas and AutoSum features.\n65\nSuper Computers-4","Periodic Worksheet-2\n(Based on chapters 5 and 6)\nA. Fill in the blanks :\n1. The _____________ holds the notification area and clock.\n2. _____________ are small pictures that contain the files.\n3. The ___________ names start with letters in a worksheet.\n4. A _______ can contain many worksheets.\nB. Write True (T) and False (F) :\n1. In Excel, you can create drawings.\n2. Windows is a GUI-based operating system.\n3. Rows are arranged vertically in a worksheet.\n4. The Recycle Bin holds the deleted files.\nC. Answer the following questions :\n1. Write the steps to change the color scheme of your computer.\n2. Write the steps to save a workbook.\n3. Define the following:\n• Worksheet\n• Cell\n• Desktop\n• Start Button\n66\nSuper Computers-4","7 More About Internet\nThe Internet is a huge network that connects computers all over the world. Using the internet, people can share information and communicate from anywhere with an internet connection.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nLearn various uses of the internet.\nUnderstand Internet-related terms.\nKnow about Internet Connection.\nMicrosoft Edge.\nNiha : Hey Tia, Do you remember about the Internet, what we have learned in our previous class?\nTia : Yes, Niha. The Internet is a big network that helps us to find information, read stories, talk to people, do shopping, play games, etc.\nNiha : That's right, Tia. It also helps us to send and receive messages too.\nTia : And it's where we can watch fun videos and talk to our friends and family, even if they're far away.\nNiha : Exactly, Tia! The Internet is like a big, friendly helper for many things we do on\nThe Internet is a global computer network, providing a variety of information and communication facilities.\nIt consists of a system of inter-connected networks, resulting in the linkage of millions of businesses, government agencies, educational institutions and individuals.\nInternet\nLAN\nWireless\n67\nSuper Computers-4","USES OF INTERNET\nIt is used to access lots of information, news and research material.\nIt allows us to communicate with others around the world.\nIt is used in banks for transactions.\nIt is used in online shopping.\nIt is used in downloading and listening to music.\nIt is used in taking a course or accessing other educational material.\nUses of Internet\nIt is used in accessing sources of entertainment and leisure such as online games, magazines and holiday planning guides.\nIt is used in accessing other computers, mobile phones and helps to exchange files.\nIt is used in sharing and editing documents with others in real time.\nTo support the above-mentioned uses and other activities, the Internet provides a variety of services that include:\nWWW (World Wide Web)\nFTP (File Transfer Protocol)\nChat rooms\nE-mail\nInstant messaging\nNewsgroups, message boards\nCONNECTING TO THE INTERNET\nYou have learned to use the internet for browsing the World Wide Web and communicate with others using email. However, to use these services you need to be connected to the internet.\n68\nSuper Computers-4","VARIOUS WAYS OF CONNECTING TO THE INTERNET\nBROADBAND\nA broadband connection allows us to use internet services that require high speed, such as playing online games or watching online videos. With a broadband connection, we remain always connected to the internet.\nWI-FI\nThe Wi-Fi connection uses radio frequency bands for connecting to the internet. It offers high speed and can be used for mobile devices, such as tablets and smartphones. It is a broadband connection without using wires or cables.\nBASIC REQUIREMENTS FOR AN INTERNET CONNECTION\nCOMPUTER SYSTEM\nThe computer system is the basic requirement for accessing the internet connection and the internet can be accessed by a desktop computer or a laptop.\nMODEM\nModem stands for modulator-demodulator. It enables a computer system to send or receive information using a telephone line. It is usually provided by the ISP. A router is used to distribute this signal to the connected devices.\nINTERNET SERVICE PROVIDER (ISP)\nTo connect your computer to the internet, you need to contact an Internet Service Provider (ISP).\nYou also need some hardware and software for the same. Let us know more about them.\nAn Internet Service Provider (ISP) is a company that offers connectivity to the Internet by charging a fee. It provides you with a username and password using which you can connect your computer or another device to the internet. When we send or receive the information on the Internet, it first reaches the ISP. The speed of your internet connection is measured in megabits per second (Mbps). The speed decides how fast your web page loads in the browser.\nBSNL, Airtel, Tata Teleservices, Verizon and Reliance Jio are a few examples of Internet Service Providers.\nINTERNET TERMINOLOGY\nLet us understand some of the common terms associated with the Internet :\n⊙ WWW or World Wide Web is a collection of interconnected pages that contain text,\n69\nSuper Computers-4","pictures, audio, and movies.\nThe various pages are connected using links and can be accessed through the Internet.\nFact Corner\nThe very first widely popular browser was Mosaic which was available from 1993 and discontinued in 1997.\nWeb pages are the pages that make up the World Wide Web. A webpage is created by using a language called HTML (HyperText Markup Language). It can be seen or accessed using a web browser. The pages may contain text, images, audio, videos and links. Phrases, such as 'Navigating the web' or 'Surfing the web' are used when we visit one webpage followed by the other.\nWeb Pages\nA Website is a collection of related web pages.\nA Browser is a software program that allows us to access and view web pages. Some examples of browsers are the Microsoft Edge, Mozilla Firefox, Google Chrome, etc.\nBrowser\nA Hyperlink (link) is an in-built connection to another related web page or a part of a web page.\nHyperlink\nURL (Uniform Resource Locator) is a web address which is written in the browser.\nURL\nWeb server is a computer that delivers (serves) web pages that we request. When you enter the address of a web page in the browser, it sends a request to the server and it organizes the connection between the address and our computer.\nWeb Server\nE-commerce (Electronic commerce) enables to buy and sell things on the internet.\nE-Commerce\nWeblog, usually shortened to blog but, occasionally spelled web log or weblog, is a web-based publication consisting of articles.\n70\nSuper Computers-4","E-mail (electronic mail) is the transmission of messages and files via a computer network. It is one of the original services on the Internet. It enables scientists and researchers working on government-sponsored projects to communicate with colleagues at other locations. Some websites provide e-mail services free of charge.\nFTP (File Transfer Protocol) is protocol that allows us to upload and download files with other computers on the Internet.\nA newsgroup is an online area in which users conduct written discussions about a particular subject. To participate in a discussion, a user sends a message to the newsgroup, and other users in the newsgroup read and reply to the message.\nChat is a typed conversation that takes place on a computer.\nInstant Messaging (IM) is an Internet communication service that notifies us when one or more people are online and then allows us to exchange messages, files or join a private chat.\nTech Corner\nWi-Fi Router is a device, such as a computer, telephone, or personal digital assistant (PDA), that can connect to the Internet.\nMICROSOFT EDGE\nMicrosoft Edge is the browser created for Windows 10. Edge replaced Internet Explorer (IE), the browser that debuted with Windows 95 and was a part of Windows operating systems for two decades. Edge is a smaller, more streamlined browser built on Web standards and designed for Web services.\nMicrosoft Edge\nREBOOT\nThis Internet is a global computer network, that links together millions of businesses, government agencies, educational institutions and individuals.\nA web page contains text, graphics, sound and video as well as in-built connections to other documents.\nE-mail (electronic mail) is the transmission of messages and files via a computer network.\nHyperlink (link) is an inbuilt connection to another related web page or a part of a Web page.\nBrowser is a software program that allows us to access and view web pages.\n71\nSuper Computers-4","BRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nWWW Internet Weblog Web Server\n1. ____________ is a global computer network.\n2. ____________ is a collection of interconnected pages.\n3. ____________ is a web-based publication consisting of articles.\n4. ____________ is a computer that delivers (serves) web pages you request.\nB. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. The website is a collection of related web pages.\n2. The Internet is a means of communication with others.\n3. ISP is optional for connecting to the Internet.\n4. Microsoft Edge is a web browser.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer.\n1. Which of the following is used for the Internet connection?\n(i) Modem (ii) MS Office\n(iii) CPU\n2. Which of the following is a web address written on the browser?\n(i) Web page (ii) URL\n(iii) Web address\n3. Which of the following enables you to sell and buy things on the Internet?\n(i) E-commerce (ii) WWW\n(iii) Newsgroup\n4. Which of the following is a typed conversation that takes place on a computer?\n(i) Web page (ii) Chat\n(iii) WWW\n72\nSuper Computers-4","D. Answer the following questions.\n1. What is FTP?\n2. What is a web page?\n3. What is a Newsgroup?\n4. What is WWW(World Wide Web)?\nEXECUTE TIME (Value Based)\nWith the help of the Internet, find out the names of any five endangered animal species. Learn about conservation efforts to protect them.\nACTIVITY TIME\n1. Make a scrap file on the topic 'Internet'.\n2. Create a chart on \"Uses of Internet\" and show it in the class.\nGroup Discussion\nDiscuss the advantages and disadvantages of the Internet.\nLet's Explore\nUse the Internet to explore the 'Seven Wonders of the World'.\nFor Educators\nIn the lab, demonstrate how to connect a computer with the Internet and discuss the terms related to the internet.\n73\nSuper Computers-4","8\nBlocks in Scratch\nConcept Note\nScratch coding is the process of writing and running programs using blocks of code. Coding means training a computer to follow the instructions given by us.\nLearning OUTCOMES\nCreating a New Project in Scratch.\nSprite's Direction.\nUnderstanding Blocks.\nNiha : Hi Tia, Do you remember that we had fun while working on Scratch?\nTia : Yes Niha! I enjoyed Scratch a lot.\nNiha : I'm glad to hear that, Tia! Scratch is amazing because it lets us use those colorful blocks to make our own games and stories.\nTia : That's true, Niha. I felt like a little programmer when we used those blocks to give instructions to the computer.\nNiha : You're absolutely right, Tia. Scratch makes it easy for us to learn about coding and be creative.\nTia : And it's so much fun. I can't wait to create more games and stories with Scratch!\nNiha : Me too! Let's explore even more and see what amazing things we can make!\nCREATING A NEW PROJECT IN SCRATCH\nWhenever you open Scratch, a new project is created automatically. However, you can create a new project at any time.\n74\nSuper Computers-4","Steps to create a new project :\n1. Click on the File menu. A drop-down menu appears.\n2. Select the New option to create a new project.\nSPRITE'S DIRECTION\nThe stage is divided into four quadrants using a horizontal line called the x-axis and a vertical line called the y-axis. The x value determines the horizontal position of the sprite and the y value determines the vertical position of the sprite. Thus, the position of a sprite on the stage is decided by its x and y values, variously called the x and y coordinates. The point at which the x and y axis meet is called the origin. The value of x and y at the origin is zero. The x and y values of the sprite can be changed by inserting the different values in the text boxes. It helps to move a sprite to a specific location on the stage.\nTech Corner\nThe x coordinate of a sprite increases from 0 to 240 when it moves to the right from the origin and decreases from 0 to -240 when it moves to the left from the origin. Similarly, the y coordinate of a sprite increases from 0 to 180 when it moves up from the origin and decreases from 0 to -180 when it moves down from the origin.\nActivity-1\nAdd any sprite on the stage. Move and place it anywhere on the stage. Note down its x and y values. Name of the selected sprite:\nx value = y value =\nUNDERSTANDING BLOCKS\nBlocks refer to the commands for the sprite. There are a number of blocks available in Scratch which are used to control the sprite on the stage. These blocks are categorised into various types such as Motion, Looks, Events, Sound, Pen, Control, etc. Each type of blocks serves a different function. Basically, these blocks provide the instructions to the sprite.\n75\nSuper Computers-4","Let's learn about these blocks in detail.\nMOTION BLOCKS\nThese blocks are used to control the movement of the sprite. You can move the sprite a number of steps backward and forward. You can also rotate it clock-wise or anti-clockwise. Some Motion blocks are shown below.\nmove\nThis block moves a sprite forward in the direction it is facing. To make the sprite move, drag the move block a number of steps into the script area. Type the number of steps to move and click on the move block. The sprite will now move by a specified number of steps.\nturn\nThis block changes the direction in which the sprite is facing. It turns a sprite according to the specified number of degrees, either clockwise or anticlockwise. This block is mainly used for rotating a sprite.\ngo to\nThis block sets the sprite's X and Y positions to the specified position, which can be either a random position or the mouse-pointer or to another sprite. Click on the drop-down arrow to select the different options.\ngo to x:() y:()\nThis block is used to move the sprite to the specified x and y positions. It is used whenever a sprite needs to jump from one position to another.\nglide () secs to ()\nThis block moves a sprite either to a random position, to the mouse-pointer or another sprite at a steady pace in the specified number of seconds. You can choose the required option using the drop-down menu. This block pauses another script running at the same time.\nglide () secs to x:() y:()\nThis block moves a sprite to the specified x and y positions in the specified number of seconds.\npoint in direction ()\nThis block rotates the sprite to point it in the specified direction. The default value is 90. It can be replaced by any number.\nif on edge, bounce\nThis block checks to see if the sprite is touching the edges of the stage, and if it does, it will make the sprite bounce back in the opposite direction.\n76\nSuper Computers-4","set rotation style ()\nset rotation style left-right\nThis block changes the rotation style of the sprite. There are three options: left-right, don't rotate and all around.\nset x to ()\nset x to 113\nThis block changes the x position of the selected sprite to a specified value.\nset y to ()\nset y to 22\nThis block changes the y position of the selected sprite to a specified value.\nExample : The following script shows how to animate the 'butterfly' sprite using the Motion blocks.\nScript : Use the 'Choose a Sprite' option and select the butterfly sprite. Use the 'Choose a Backdrop' option and select the forest backdrop.\nwhen this sprite clicked\nforever\ngo to random position\nglide 1 secs to mouse-pointer\npoint in direction 90\nif on edge, bounce\nScript\nOutput\nExplanation : When the user clicks on the butterfly, it goes to the random position. Then, it glides to the mouse pointer for 1 second and rotates to point it to 90 degrees. If the butterfly touches the edges of the stage, it bounces back in the opposite direction. This script will run continuously in a loop.\nActivity-2\n1. Select the 'Bat' sprite.\n2. Select the 'Night City' backdrop.\n3. Use the glide block in combination with other blocks to glide the bat. Make sure it does not disappear from the stage.\nLOOKS BLOCKS\nLooks blocks are used to change the look and appearance of a sprite. You can also apply several effects to your sprite using Looks blocks.\n77\nSuper Computers-4","Some of the Looks blocks to apply effects are described below.\nsay\nThis block is used to show a speech callout. Example, Hi! The speech bubble will play until another speech or thought block is placed in the script below it or the stop sign is pressed.\nthink\nThis block is used to show a thought bubble callout. Example, Hmm... The thought a bubble will play until another speech or thought block is placed in the script below it or the stop sign is pressed.\nHide\nThis block is used to hide a sprite.\nshow\nThis block is used to show the hidden sprite.\nswitch costume to ()\nThis block is used to change the costume of a sprite. You can choose a particular costume using the drop-down menu.\nTech Corner\nIn Scratch, there are three tabs, Code, Costume and Sounds. The Code tab comprises of Blocks palette which has all the available blocks in Scratch and allows you to drag blocks into the Script area. The Costume tab contains a list of costumes for the selected sprite. You can change the appearance of a sprite by changing its costume. The Sounds tab contains various audio clips.\nSOUND BLOCKS\nThese blocks are used to make sprite play sounds. Some Sound blocks are shown below.\nplay sound () until done\nThis block is used to play the selected sound completely and only then run other blocks below it.\nstart sound ()\nThis block is used to play the sound and continue to execute the next block without waiting for the sound to finish playing.\nset volume to () %\nThis block allows you to set the volume of the sound. The value is added in the box. It is only applicable for the sprite or stage that has the block in its script.\n78\nSuper Computers-4","stop all sounds\nstop all sounds\nThis block is used to stop playing all the sounds.\nFact\nCorner\nYou can create a script to play more than one sound. You can also play a sound multiple times using Control blocks.\nExample 3 : The script shows how to set the volume when a key is pressed. Script:\nwhen down arrow key pressed\nset volume to 5 %\nplay sound Meow until done\nExplanation: When the user presses the down-arrow key, the volume is set to 5% and the 'cat' sprite will play the 'meow' sound completely.\nActivity-3\nPerform the following tasks.\n1. Add the 'cat' sprite.\n2. Play the 'cat' sound when you click the Go icon.\nPEN BLOCK\nPen block has the blocks that allow you to draw objects on the stage. The Pen blocks are green in colour.\nUSING PEN BLOCKS\nTo use Pen blocks, you need to add them to your project.\nFollow the given steps to add Pen blocks :\n1. Click on the Code tab.\n2. Click on the Add Extension.\n3. Select the Pen block from the list.\nThe Pen blocks will be added to the project.\nAdd Extension\n79\nSuper Computers-4","Let us learn about them.\nclear\nIt clears all pen marks from the stage.\npen down\nIt puts the pen down and draws lines while moving.\npen up\nIt lifts the pen up and does not draw lines while moving.\nset pen color to\nIt sets the colour of the pen chosen by the programmer.\nset pen size to 1\nIt sets the thickness of the pen by typing a new number in the number area.\nstamp\nIt is used to make a duplicate copy of the sprite.\nExample 1.: Scratch program to draw a line\nStep 1: Open Scratch.\nStep 2: Click on the Pen block and drag the pen down block in the script area.\nStep 3: Click on the change pen color by 10 block and drag it to the script area just below the previous block to attach it.\nwhen clicked\npen down\nchange pen color by 10\nmove 20 steps\nStep 4: From the Motion blocks, click on the move 10 steps block and drag it to the script area. Replace the value of steps from 10 to 20.\nYou can see that a colourful line is drawn. Increase the value in block to draw a bigger line.\n80\nSuper Computers-4","Example 2: Scratch program to draw a square\nStep 1: Open Scratch.\nStep 2: Click on the Pen block and drag the pen down block in the script area.\nStep 3: Click on the change pen color by 10 block and drag it to the script area just below the previous block to attach It.\nStep 4: From the Motion blocks, click on the move 10 steps block and drag it to the script area. Replace the value of steps from 10 to 20.\nStep 5: Click on the turn 15 degrees and drag it just below the previous block to attach both the blocks. Replace the value of steps from 15 to 90.\nStep 6: Click on the first block 4 times.\nwhen clicked / pen down / change pen color by 10 / move 60 steps / turn 90 degrees\nSquare is Drawn\nYou can see that a colorful square is drawn. Increase the value in the block to draw a bigger square.\nExample 3: A Scratch program to draw a circle.\nStep 1: Open Scratch.\nStep 2: Click on the Pen block and drag the pen down block in the script area.\nStep 3: Click on the change pen color by 10 block and drag it to the script area just below the previous block to attach It.\nStep 4: Click the repeat 10 block and replace the value of 10 with 360.\nStep 5: Click on the turn 15 degrees and drag It just below the previous block to attach both the blocks. Replace the value of steps from 15 to 90.\nStep 6: From the Motion blocks, click on the move 10 steps the block and drag It to the script area. Replace the value of 10 to 1.\nStep 7: Click on the first block.\n81\nSuper Computers-4","You can see that a colorful circle is drawn. Try by changing repeat to 90 and moving by 4 and turning by 4. This will create the circle faster.\nA Scratch, a script is created for a sprite. It is the only object that can move, so every project has at least one sprite.\nThe stage is divided into four quadrants using a horizontal line called the x-axis and a vertical line called the y-axis.\nThere are a number of blocks available in Scratch used to control the sprite on the stage.\nThese blocks are categorised into various types such as Motion, Looks, Events, Sound, Pen, Control, etc.\nYou can move the sprite a number of steps backward and forward and also rotate it clockwise or anti-clockwise using Motion blocks.\nLooks blocks are used to change the look and appearance of a sprite. You can also apply several effects to your sprite using Looks blocks.\nSound blocks are used to make sprite play sounds.\nBRAIN BOOSTER\nA. Fill in the blanks with the help of the words given in the box.\nsound, motion, looks, turn\n1. The _______________ block is used to move the sprite.\n2. The _______________ block rotates the sprite to point it in the specified direction.\n3. The _______________ blocks are used to make the sprite play sounds.\n4. The _______________ blocks are used to change the appearance of a sprite.\nB. Write true (T) or false (F).\n1. The Motion blocks are used to run the script.\n2. Looks blocks are used to show or hide the sprite.\n3. The x-axis and y-axis tell the direction of a sprite.\n4. You can insert only one sprite in a script at a time.\n82\nSuper Computers-4","C. Answer the following questions:\n1. Write the name and purpose of any two motion blocks.\n2. Name any two Looks blocks.\n3. Which block is used to add sound in Scratch?\nEXECUTE TIME\n1. Raina has created a scene of animals in Scratch. Now she wants to add sound of animals. Which block would you suggest her to do the same?\n2. Tiana wants to create a scene of talking friends. But she does not know how to do it. Can you help her in her task?\nACTIVITY TIME\n1. Create a scene of dancing kids on the stage.\n2. Create an animation scene of animals in a jungle. Also add sounds for animals.\nGroup Discussion\nDiscuss various types of blocks and their purpose available in Scratch.\nLet's Explore\nExplore the use of variables in Scratch.\nFor Educators\nDemonstrate to the students all the blocks explained in this chapter.\n83\nSuper Computers-4","Periodic Worksheet-3\n(Based on chapters 7 and 8)\nA. Fill in the blanks :\n1. The etiquettes of the Internet are called ____________.\n2. Never give your ____________ information to anyone.\n3. The ____________ block moves the sprite to any random position on the stage.\n4. The __________ block is used to stop playing all the sounds.\nB. Define the following:\n1. Internet Netiquettes\n2. Internet\n3. Sprite\n4. Pen blocks\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer:\n1. Which of the following is used for the Internet connection?\n(i) Modem (ii) MS Office (iii) CPU\n2. Which of the following is a web address written on the browser?\n(i) Web page (ii) URL (iii) Web address\n3. Which of the following is a typed conversation that takes place on a computer?\n(i) Web page (ii) Chat (iii) WWW\n4. Which of the following removes the shapes from the stage?\n(i) Pen Up (ii) Clear (iii) Pen down\n84\nSuper Computers-4","ROBOTICS AND AI\nINTRODUCTION TO ROBOTICS\nRobotics is a technology for designing and implementing robots. Robots are machines which are programmed to perform tasks just like humans. They perform tasks upon receiving instructions or activate on their own using inbuilt memory.\nThe idea behind creating this intelligent machine is to ease the workload of humans.\nRobot\nRobots are designed to perform tasks such as :\nMoving from one place to another\nPackaging things\nMar Rover Wheels\nRecognizing things\nModifying objects\nCHARACTERISTICS OF ROBOTS\nA robot has some characteristics :\n1. Mechanical Construction : A robot has some sort of mechanical construction which enables it to operate in an environment for which it is designed.\nFor example : Mar Rover Wheels is a robot whose legs are made up of titanium tubing and wheels are made up of aluminium. These robots can drive over knee-high rocks up to 40 cm.\nPicking objects\n85\nSuper Computers-4","2. Electrical Components : In robots, electrical components are needed to power and control the machinery. When the batteries of robots run out of power, they need to return to their docking station to recharge their batteries.\nFor example : An electric current is required to power the battery of the robot.\nElectrical Components\n3. Computer Programming : Computer programming is needed to instruct the robot what to do and how to perform the task. A robot is a machine that depends on human instructions to operate. Therefore, to instruct it, computer programs are created and installed in robots according to the environment or field for which it is designed.\nRoomba\nFor example : Roomba is a robot which is a vacuum floor cleaning system. It is designed to clean and vacuum the floors.\nROLE OF AI IN ROBOTICS\n1. Robotics is a process of building robots and Artificial Intelligence is a process of putting intelligence in robots through programming.\n2. Artificial Intelligence plays an important role in making robots intelligent.\n3. Artificially Intelligent robots can perform tasks such as recognizing and analyzing objects, picking and moving objects and even modifying objects.\nAI in Robotics\n4. Artificial Intelligence gives a vision to the robot using which it can see, identify and analyze objects.\n5. Artificially Intelligent robots can detect and recognize faces and expressions as well.\nUSES OF ROBOT IN SOCIETY\nRobots are used to ease the workload of humans in various areas, like:\n1. Manufacturing\nRobots are largely used in manufacturing industries Such robots are called Industrial Robots. These robots are specially trained to perform the tedious and repetitive tasks like.\n86\nSuper Computers-4","Welding\nAssembling\nPainting\nFor example : SCARA (Selective Compliance Articulated Robot Arm) is an industrial robot. It is used in manufacturing.\nMachine tending\nMachine cutting\nGrinding and polishing\n2. Health\nRobots are used in health care to assist physicians in performing various operations such as :\nProsthetic limbs disabled patients\nProvide therapy to patients\nMass production of drugs and medicines\nPerforming surgeries\n87\nSuper Computers-4","For example : Da Vinci surgical system is a robot which is used by surgeons for performing robotic-assisted surgeries.\n3. Education\nRobots are used in education industry to perform the following tasks :\nFor example : Nao is an educational humanoid robot which is used in over 6000 educational institutes around the world.\n4. Transport\nRobots are used as modes of transportation as well. They are equipped with lasers, sensors, GPS (Global Positioning Systems) and other technology. Transport robots are also used in various factories, restaurants, medical institutions, and other fields to carry goods. Some examples of the use of robots in transport are :\nFor example : Waymo is a driver-less vehicle which is available 24/7.\n5. Taking Pictures\nThe robot photographers are used for taking pictures with the help of a camera which is mounted on the robot. It can work 24/7 without any factors that impact human photographers like weather or sickness. The robot rotates towards the group of people and then back to the line taking pictures continuously from different angles. It performs the task of photography much faster and with greater accuracy than human photographers.\nFor example : A selfie bot is a robot photographer which is 6 feet tall roaming around the crowd and chatting with people. It captures their moments with printable pictures.\nRobot Photographer\n88\nSuper Computers-4","GAME NAME-SONG MAKER USINGGOOGLE EXPERIMENTS\nLet's learn to create our own song using google experiments\nStep 1 : Open google experiments in your chrome browser\nLink- https://experiments.withgoogle.com/\nexperiments.withgoogle.com\nLooking for Google's latest AI experiments? Find out more at g.co/Labs\nExperiments with Google\nCollections\nExperiments\nSearch\nSUBMIT EXPERIMENT\nCOLLECTION\nTensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers\nA collection of experiments that showcases the possibilities of building with TensorFlow Lite for Microcontrollers.\nVIEW COLLECTION\nStep 2 : Scroll down and click on the \"Experiments for Learning\"\nLooking for Google's latest AI experiments? Find out more at g.co/Labs\nExperiments with Google\nCollections\nExperiments\nSearch\nSUBMIT EXPERIMENT\nWebXR Experiments\nAI Experiments\nCulture Experiments\nExperiments for Learning\nStart With One\nChrome Experiments\n89\nSuper Computers-4","Step 3 : Now click on the \"Launch song maker\" button\nStep 4 : The following screen will open\nStep 5 : Click on the boxes to make your own song music. One example is shown below\nStep 6 : Click on the play button to hear the created music\n90\nSuper Computers-4","Project\n1. Prepare a chart on the topic \"Computer Lab Etiquettes\" by collecting the pictures from magazines, newspapers or the internet.\n2. Make a collage on \"Input/Output and Memory Devices\". Collect the picture from magazines, newspapers or internet with the help of parents.\n3. Name any 5 websites for the students of your class (means class 4th) which are considered best for studying online about computers. Give two reasons for choosing the websites on an A4 sheet.\n4. Search the web and write 10 ways to keep yourself healthy during COVID-19 on an A4 sheet. Mention the names of the websites used for collecting the data.\n5. Make a Word document in MS Word about \"Myself\" and also do formatting in it of your choice.\n6. You must be reading the newspaper daily. Collect the prices of CDs, Keyboards, Printers, CPU, and Mouse from the IT supplement which comes weekly with every newspaper.\n7. Create a model of our earth using the waste CDs of your home. You can get help from the internet.\n8. Write a poem on \"My Mother\" in MS Word (10 lines) with a beautiful picture get it printed with your name and class written on the document.\n• Change the font of the text to \"Comic Sans MS\".\n• Align the main heading to the center.\n• Give a page border to the document.\n• Change the font style of the heading to bold.\n9. Search some of the examples of artificial intelligence from internet. Grab some pictures and prepare a chart with some meaningful information.\n10. Is there any difference between scratch 2.0 and scratch 3.0. Collect the information from magazines, books, the internet or newspaper. Make an A4 sheet with all the information.\n91\nSuper Computers-4","Test Paper-2\n(Based on chapters 5 to 8)\nA. Fill in the blanks :\n1. The word \"Google\" is a _______________.\n2. The sprite is the graphical image of _______________.\n3. The difference between animals and humans is _______________.\n4. By default the first slide of a presentation is selected as _______________.\n5. A computer follows stepwise instructions to complete any task, which is known as _______________.\nB. Write True (T) and False (F) :\n1. Every website has a name.\n2. Earlier Microsoft Edge was Internet Explorer.\n3. AI machines have moral and emotional values.\n4. Script is an object that performs various actions on the stage.\n5. A slide is like a page of PowerPoint presentation that displays some information.\nC. Tick (✓) the right answer:\n1. The term AI was first used by _______________.\n(i) John McCarthy (ii) Williams (iii) Berner Lee\n2. _______________ block contains the set of blocks, which are used to draw the objects on the stage.\n(i) Motion (ii) Pen (iii) Palette\n3. _______________ is the area where sprite performs the actions.\n(i) Scratch (ii) Block (iii) Stage\n92\nSuper Computers-4","4. _______________ is a collection of interconnected pages.\n(i) Internet (ii) Website (iii) Microsoft Edge\n5. AI machines are best suited for _______________ jobs.\n(i) Human (ii) Monkey (iii) Computer\nD. Answer the following questions :\n1. What is Computer Etiquette?\n2. Write about two negative aspects of the Internet.\n3. What are the uses of Robotics in different fields?\n4. Name the four view options available in MS PowerPoint.\n5. Is there any difference between Scratch 2.0 and Scratch 3.0?\nE. Match the following :\nColumn 'A' Column 'B'\n1. Scratch (i) Artificial Intelligence\n2. MS PowerPoint (ii) Page in a slide show\n3. AI (iii) Programming Language\n4. Microsoft Edge (iv) Presentation Software\n5. Slide (v) Internet Explorer\n93\nSuper Computers-4","Cyber Olympiad Paper\n1. 1024 bytes equals _________.\n(i) 1 Kilobyte (KB)\t(ii) 1 Megabyte (MB)\n(iii) 1 Gigabyte (GB)\t(iv) 1 Character\n2. A hard disk can store upto _________.\n(i) 2 Terabyte\t(ii) 2 Gigabyte\n(iii) 2 Megabyte\t(iv) 2 Kilobyte\n3. How many types of ROM are there?\n(i) 3\t(ii) 4\n(iii) 2\t(iv) 1\n4. _________ is used for reading/writing information from a CD.\n(i) CD drive\t(ii) Pen drive\n(iii) Hard drive\t(iv) None\n5. Which icon is used to connect to the internet?\n(i) Network\t(ii) Microsoft Edge\n(iii) Recycle Bin\t(iv) All\n6. By which icon we can easily restore our deleted files?\n(i) This PC\t(ii) Recycle Bin\n(iii) Network\t(iv) My Documents\n7. In Windows, files are stored in locations called _________.\n(i) Storage\t(ii) Programs\n(iii) Folders\t(iv) Stacks\n8. A predefined combination of font style, colour, size of text that can be applied to the selected text.\n(i) Font\t(ii) Style\n(iii) Format\t(iv) None\n94\nSuper Computers-4","9. A __________ box is an object which acts as a placeholder to enter text.\n(i) Colour (ii) Text\n(iii) Check (iv) None\n10. __________ is a feature in Microsoft Word that is used to insert attractive text in a document.\n(i) ClipArt (ii) SmartArt\n(iii) WordArt (iv) ViewArt\n11. Which settings can you select when adding a quick table in Microsoft Word?\n(i) Only the number of rows and columns\n(ii) Rows, columns, height and width.\n(iii) Rows, columns and design\n(iv) Rows, columns, Autofit and format\n12. To adjust the column widths using a specific measurement, you would:\n(i) Drag the column markers on the ruler\n(ii) Use the table properties dialog box\n(iii) Drag a vertical line on a column\n(iv) Use the Autofit feature\n13. Merging cells means\n(i) Combining (ii) Separating\n(iii) Adjusting (iv) None\n14. Which method should you use to get to borders and shading?\n(i) Right Click\n(ii) Format-Borders and Shading\n(iii) Double click the 4 point cursor in the left hand curser of the table and select borders and shading\n(iv) None\n95\nSuper Computers-4","15. How many rows and columns in a table in PowerPoint?\n(i) 65 rows, 65 columns (ii) 75 rows, 75 columns\n(iii) 70 rows, 70 columns (iv) 80 rows, 80 columns\n16. Which of the following is the action button in PowerPoint?\n(i) End (ii) Help\n(iii) Home (iv) All of above\n17. Which toolbar has a slide design option in MS PowerPoint?\n(i) Drawing Toolbar (ii) Standard Toolbar\n(iii) Formatting Toolbar (iv) None of the above\n18. Animation schemes can be applied to __________ in the presentation.\n(i) All Slides (ii) Select Slides\n(iii) Current Slide (iv) All of the above\n19. Which tab is not available on left panel when you open a presentation?\n(i) Outline (ii) Slides\n(iii) Notes (iv) All of the above\n20. Which of the following sections does not exist in a slide layout?\n(i) Titles (ii) Lists\n(iii) Animation (iv) None of above\n96\nSuper Computers-4"]}